\n70\t    E007[\"E007 Impl: Platform Operator CI FIPS regression gate\"]\n71\t    E001 --> E003\n72\t    E002 --> E004\n73\t    E002 --> E005\n74\t    E003 --> E005\n75\t    E003 --> E007\n76\t    E002 --> E006\n77\t    E003 --> E006\n78\t    E004 --> E006\n79\t    E005 --> E006\n80\t    E007 --> E006\n81\t```\n82\t\n83\t## DAG Justification\n84\t\n85\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n86\t|------|------|-----------|\n87\t| E001 → E003 | Step 2 new-component detection / Rule 3 | Platform Operator has no architecture context file; its platform status, image set, and crypto posture are unsubstantiated. The investigation's outcome rewrites or removes the remediation epic — a true gate (`rewrite`), recorded via `gated_by` on E003. The outcome space is an open-ended per-image inventory (not ≤3 bounded outcomes), so no conditional branch files; the gate carries a fallback approach instead. |\n88\t| E002 → E004 | Implementation ordering (gating semantics) | HLR-3 requires a *gating* check that fails KServe builds that *reintroduce* non-validated crypto. Enabling it before a remediated baseline exists would fail every KServe serving-stack build. This is a semantic ordering, not a Rule 15 merge-conflict coordination case. |\n89\t| E003 → E007 | Implementation ordering (gating semantics) | Same gating semantics for the Platform Operator side of HLR-3: the check is wired into the Platform Operator build pipeline confirmed by the E001 investigation (a transitive prerequisite via E003, which is gated by E001) and needs the remediated Platform Operator baseline to pass. Kept as a separate epic from E004 because it is a different component/team tuple (Platform Operator / RHOAI Platform) with its own build pipeline. |\n90\t| E002 → E005 | Artifact dependency | The evidence package contains build attestations and test results for the remediated KServe images; it cannot be assembled before they exist. |\n91\t| E003 → E005 | Artifact dependency | Same, for the Platform Operator images in the evidence scope. |\n92\t| E002, E003, E004, E005, E007 → E006 | Rule 11 | `docs-authoring` is blocked by all Implementation epics; docs describe what was built. Per the docs-authoring priority exception these edges do not trigger priority inheritance. |\n93\t| (no edge) E001 ∥ E002 | Rule 14 | Different components/repos, no shared artifacts — KServe remediation starts immediately and is not gated by the Platform Operator validation. |\n94\t| (no edge) E004 ∥ E005 | Rule 14 | After their respective prerequisites, the CI regression gate and the evidence package share no artifact; the CI gate is not certification evidence. |\n95\t| (no edge) E004 ∥ E007 | Rule 14 | The two CI gates target different components' build pipelines in different repos, with no shared artifact; the Platform Operator gate may reuse the KServe check's detection *approach* (knowledge transfer, not an artifact dependency), so neither blocks the other. |\n96\t\n97\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n98\t\n99\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abridged) | Epics |\n100\t|-----|----------|------------------------|-------|\n101\t| HLR-1 | P0 | All serving-stack images run on FIPS-enabled clusters using validated crypto modules | E002, E003 (gated by E001) |\n102\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Compliance evidence package assembled and submitted through the security review board to the external certification lab | E005 |\n103\t| HLR-3 | P1 | CI enforces FIPS regressions with a gating check | E004 (KServe builds), E007 (Platform Operator builds, gated transitively by E001 via E003) |\n104\t| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation states FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites | E006 |\n105\t\n106\tAll HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements.\n107\t\n108\t## Tiered Delivery\n109\t\n110\tThe external certification lab (six-to-nine-month historical turnaround) and\n111\tthe security review board cadence are third-party schedules that engineering\n112\tcannot compress. Partial value without certification: yes.\n113\t\n114\t- **Tier 1** (independent value): E001, E002, E003, E004, E007 — the serving\n115\t  stack actually runs on FIPS-enabled clusters with validated crypto, and\n116\t  regressions are enforced in CI across both serving-stack build pipelines\n117\t  (KServe and Platform Operator).\n118\t- **Tier 2** (externally gated): E005 — evidence package assembly and\n119\t  submission; the certification outcome and timing are externally controlled.\n120\t  The lab is a third party that resolves the certification itself, so it is\n121\t  modeled as a precondition/schedule risk on E005 (noted in its acceptance\n122\t  criteria), not as a separate epic.\n123\t\n124\t## Health Warnings\n125\t\n126\t1. **Missing strategy sections** — no Impacted Teams, Dependencies, Acceptance\n127\t   Criteria, or NFR sections. Team assignments for Platform Operator\n128\t   (\"RHOAI Platform\") and Documentation (\"RHOAI Documentation\") are assumptions;\n129\t   KServe's team (RHOAI Serving) comes from architecture context.\n130\t2. **Ambiguity — serving data plane scope**: the problem statement says \"KServe\n131\t   components and the serving data plane\" link non-validated crypto, and\n132\t   KServe's architecture context lists model-server runtimes (vLLM, OpenVINO,\n133\t   Caikit) as its model-execution dependency. \"Serving Runtimes\" is NOT in the\n134\t   Affected Components table. The decomposition scopes the KServe epic to\n135\t   KServe-owned control-plane and data-plane images (controller,\n136\t   predictor/transformer/explainer, queue proxy, storage initializer, router).\n137\t   If model-server runtime images are intended to be in scope for HLR-1 (\"all\n138\t   serving-stack images\"), the strategy needs an amendment — they sit in the\n139\t   request path per architecture context.\n140\t3. **Priority note — docs epic**: HLR-4 is P2, but per the docs-authoring\n141\t   priority exception the docs epic derives its priority from the strategy\n142\t   frontmatter (`priority: Critical`) → P0. Flagged so the inversion against\n143\t   the HLR label is visible; it does not push priority upstream to\n144\t   implementations.\n145\t4. **External schedule risk**: lab turnaround and review-board cadence dominate\n146\t   the schedule (per the strategy's own note); the tiered delivery above\n147\t   mitigates by decoupling Tier 1 value from the certification timeline.\n148\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9ace5612da984093","agent_id":"acd2df83908752a59","session_id":"f6f494ff-e064-4d81-8a07-c93e866aadc4","uuid":"f8f68570-4b94-40ad-ab4c-bd9b90ec1318","timestamp":"2026-10-21T16:15:24.092Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b06d0a3b4252c66a","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9013 decomposition"}
